{"name":"Don Juan","hex":"#5a4f51","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Don Juan","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A man who obsessively seduces women."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Don_Juan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Don Juan","blocks":[{"text":"Don Juan, also known as Don Giovanni (Italian), is a legendary fictional Spanish libertine who devotes his life to seducing women."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Max_Slevogt_-_Der_S%C3%A4nger_Francisco_d%27Andrade_als_Don_Giovanni_in_Mozarts_Oper_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Max_Slevogt_-_Der_S%C3%A4nger_Francisco_d%27Andrade_als_Don_Giovanni_in_Mozarts_Oper_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Juan"},{"title":"Don Juan (poem)","blocks":[{"text":"Don Juan is an English unfinished satirical epic poem written by Lord Byron between 1819 and 1824 that portrays the Spanish folk legend of Don Juan, not as a womaniser as historically portrayed, but as a victim easily seduced by women. Don Juan is a poem written in ottava rima and presented in 16 cantos in which Lord Byron derived the character of Don Juan from traditional Spanish folk legends; however, the story was very much his own. Upon publication in 1819, cantos I and II were widely criticised as immoral because Byron had so freely ridiculed the social subjects and public figures of his time. At his death in 1824, Lord Byron had completed 16 of 17 cantos, whilst canto XVII remained unfinished."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Don_Juan_1st_edition_cover%2C_1819.png/330px-Don_Juan_1st_edition_cover%2C_1819.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Juan_(poem)"},{"title":"Juan","blocks":[{"text":"Juan is a given name, the Spanish and Manx versions of John. The name is of Hebrew origin and has the meaning \"God has been gracious.\" It is very common in Spain and in other Spanish-speaking countries around the world and in the Philippines, and also in the Isle of Man. The name is popular around the world and can be pronounced differently according the region. In Spanish, the diminutive form is Juanito, with feminine form Juana, and feminine diminutive Juanita."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Simmler_Wilhelm_Troszel.jpg/500px-Simmler_Wilhelm_Troszel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Simmler_Wilhelm_Troszel.jpg/500px-Simmler_Wilhelm_Troszel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of Wilhelm Troszel as Don Juan, by Józef Simmler, 1846","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASimmler_Wilhelm_Troszel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Dos_Hermanas_-_Parque_de_La_Alquer%C3%ADa%2C_Monumento_a_Don_Juan_Tenorio_3.jpg/500px-Dos_Hermanas_-_Parque_de_La_Alquer%C3%ADa%2C_Monumento_a_Don_Juan_Tenorio_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Dos_Hermanas_-_Parque_de_La_Alquer%C3%ADa%2C_Monumento_a_Don_Juan_Tenorio_3.jpg/1280px-Dos_Hermanas_-_Parque_de_La_Alquer%C3%ADa%2C_Monumento_a_Don_Juan_Tenorio_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Dos_Hermanas_-_Parque_de_La_Alquer%C3%ADa%2C_Monumento_a_Don_Juan_Tenorio_3.jpg/500px-Dos_Hermanas_-_Parque_de_La_Alquer%C3%ADa%2C_Monumento_a_Don_Juan_Tenorio_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Monument to Don Juan Tenorio in Dos Hermanas, Seville","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADo